WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.200
As we define our goals and Key Performance&nbsp;
Indicators, or KPIs, for whatever business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:16.960
or personal case, we need to make sure we’re&nbsp;
actually creating objectives that matter. Goals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:24.160
objectives, and KPIs are tools. They have a use.&nbsp;
They need to be designed and used with intention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:28.720
and a best practice of defining goals&nbsp;
and KPIs is using the SMART criteria.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.080
In this video, we’ll define the SMART&nbsp;
criteria with relatable examples and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.600
provide some best practice guidelines&nbsp;
for making your own SMART goals.

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:42.240
What are SMART goals?
SMART goals are ones that set objectives and KPIs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:49.120
in a precise and easy to communicate way. First&nbsp;
described in print 1981 by author George T. Doran&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.840
and later expanded by Professor Robert S.&nbsp;
Ruben, SMART is an acronym that stands for:

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.840
• Specific
• Measurable&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.320
• Achievable
• Realistic&nbsp;

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.160
• Time-bound

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.280
SMART states that clear, attainable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:10.080
strategic goals are the most effective way&nbsp;
to create concrete milestones and metrics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:15.440
Instead of a general goal like “increase&nbsp;
sales,” we might consider a SMART goal like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.760
“increase February’s year-over-year premium&nbsp;
subscription sales in California by 4%.”

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:26.000
SMART goals are the individual steps of a&nbsp;
well-considered strategy to achieving larger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.120
objectives. If you were running a marathon&nbsp;
or climbing a mountain, SMART goals would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:35.360
be the mile markers along the way, letting you&nbsp;
know where you are and how well you’re doing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.840
It isn’t just about getting to the end,&nbsp;
but rather what steps to take to get there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:45.760
SMART KPIs are what SMART goals use as the&nbsp;
accountable measurements, with a start and end.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:50.640
Specific
Being clear about your goals and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:56.720
expectations is the first step to reaching them.&nbsp;
When goals are too wide or vague, they are easy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:03.200
miss. If the definition is too broad, they become&nbsp;
harder to measure and also harder to achieve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:08.320
Being too vague allows for a lot&nbsp;
of error and misunderstanding.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:13.440
It’s like climbing a mountain not knowing how&nbsp;
long the path is, how long it’s supposed to take,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.240
what the conditions are, or even&nbsp;
what to wear while doing it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:22.880
With specific goals, you can see a path&nbsp;
form from start to finish. Specific KPIs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:27.920
define the path even further with benchmarks,&nbsp;
ensuring everyone knows exactly where you are.

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.000
When you create your SMART goal,&nbsp;
try to answer questions like:

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:37.520
• What are the concrete steps in our plan?
• Who is necessary to make it happen?&nbsp;

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:43.760
• Where will our efforts take place?
• Why is this valuable to our long-term strategy?&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.680
• How do we prioritize this&nbsp;
goal compared to other goals?

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:54.720
Example of a Specific SMART goal: If Superman&nbsp;
is trying to “save the world,” then he’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:58.880
going to be more specific by narrowing the&nbsp;
objective to “prevent Lex Luthor’s plan.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:04.720
This helps Superman be a lot more focused in&nbsp;
his goals and helps him prioritize stopping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.720 --> 00:03:08.720
a supervillain over saving the endangered&nbsp;
ivory-billed woodpecker from extinction.

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.200
Measurable
If you can’t measure something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.240
you can’t tell if you’re performing well or if&nbsp;
you’re off track and need to course-correct.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.720
In addition to being specific in your goals,&nbsp;
you need to be able to quantify your data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:27.120
or indicate where you are progressing&nbsp;
towards your goal. Setting measurable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.240
goals and criteria also lets everyone involved&nbsp;
evaluate their performance and stay on track.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:36.320
When you create your SMART goal,&nbsp;
try to answer questions like:

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:40.720
• What data do we need?
• Where will our data live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.600
and how will we access it?
• Is our data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:47.680
reliable and verifiable?
• What are reasonable milestones?&nbsp;

00:03:47.680 --> 00:03:53.760
• How much is enough and how much is not enough?
• How will we know we’ve accomplished our goal?

00:03:55.440 --> 00:04:01.120
Example of a Measurable SMART goal: If your&nbsp;
goal is to climb a mountain in a day, then you’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:05.840
likely do some research and plan out how many&nbsp;
miles to hike per hour and track your progress.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:09.600
You would then measure your distance&nbsp;
and time according to your goal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:14.240
This is a much SMARTer plan than instead&nbsp;
looking up to the top of the mountain in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:17.840
the afternoon and just starting to&nbsp;
walk, hoping to the top by sundown.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.440
Achievable
It’s important to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:26.560
at your end objectives and plan out goals that are&nbsp;
within realistic capabilities. It’s also important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:31.360
not to get too carried away and use far-fetched&nbsp;
or unreachable goals as motivators, constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:37.280
chasing an impossibly lofty goal. Allow yourself,&nbsp;
and your stakeholders, to feel motivated by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:42.560
satisfaction of achievement and real progress.&nbsp;
When setting goals, be ambitious and push for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.920
success, but also understand internal and external&nbsp;
limits. Achievement needs to be sustainable.

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.840
When you create your SMART goal,&nbsp;
try to answer questions like:

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.800
• What are the necessary&nbsp;
steps to achieve this goal?&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:58.320
• How much direct control do we&nbsp;
have over achieving this goal?&nbsp;

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.920
• Is this goal realistic&nbsp;
compared to previous performance?&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:06.240
• What is the precedent?
• Why do we think this goal is achievable?&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:10.960
• What external (and internal) factors&nbsp;
might prevent us from achieving this goal?

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.240
Example of an Achievable SMART goal:&nbsp;
Search-and-rescue dogs’ have a singular purpose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:21.680
to find survivors of disasters like earthquakes&nbsp;
or terrorism attacks. In order to keep the dogs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:27.040
motivated in especially fatal disaster areas, dog&nbsp;
handlers will sometimes hide “fake” survivors for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:32.080
the dogs to rescue so the dogs will be motivated&nbsp;
to continue their hard work. The gratification&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:35.760
of achievement empowers and sustains the&nbsp;
continued good behavior of achievement.

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:41.600
Realistic (and Relevant)
Similar to the achievable criteria above,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.240
goals also need to be realistic and relevant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.200 --> 00:05:49.440
Setting goals that are completely out of reach&nbsp;
won’t make your team strive to reach harder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:53.040
but at the same time setting goals that&nbsp;
are too easy won’t make your team better.

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:58.640
It’s also important to make sure your goals are&nbsp;
relevant to your goals or business objectives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:04.960
over time. KPIs need to measure your business&nbsp;
goals over short-term and long-term strategies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:11.280
and it’s ok to adjust criteria as time passes.&nbsp;
What works now won’t always work the same later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:17.360
and it’s important to identify when business needs&nbsp;
change. Stay aware of your long-term goals, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:22.320
don’t be afraid to readjust the short-term tactics&nbsp;
that will help achieve those long-term plans.

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:26.400
When you create your SMART goal,&nbsp;
try to answer questions like:

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:31.440
• Why is this the goal now?
• Why wasn’t this the goal in the past?&nbsp;

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:37.840
• Who are the right people to pursue this goal?
• How will this goal advance a long-term strategy?&nbsp;

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:41.920
• What is the anticipated&nbsp;
return on investment (ROI)?&nbsp;

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:45.040
• What would it mean if we&nbsp;
failed to reach this goal?

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:51.920
Example of a Realistic SMART goal: If your&nbsp;
ultimate goal is to climb Mount Everest but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:56.880
you’ve never climbed a whole mountain before, it&nbsp;
would be unrealistic to head straight to Everest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.760 --> 00:07:02.160
The criteria suggested above indicate that you&nbsp;
should first be asking yourself why you should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.160 --> 00:07:06.800
trying to hike Mount Everest at all, if you're&nbsp;
the right person to be hiking Mount Everest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:11.520
and what it would mean if you didn't make&nbsp;
it to the top. If you decided to proceed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.840
you then would need to plot out a&nbsp;
realistic training schedule for the feat.

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:19.120
Time-bound
The final letter of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:23.680
SMART is often overlooked, but it’s incredibly&nbsp;
important to have a time-frame for your goals.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:29.920
A time-bound goal can consist of either start and&nbsp;
end-points or a collection of timed parameters or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.920 --> 00:07:35.200
milestones. You may have a deadline to reach&nbsp;
certain objectives in order to stay on track.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:40.240
Or you may want to track metrics across&nbsp;
a period of time, whether it’s daily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:45.920
monthly, or quarterly. Whatever you choose,&nbsp;
plan ahead of time to make sure your KPIs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.920 --> 00:07:50.000
can be measured as time passes in order for&nbsp;
you to reach your goals in a timely manner.

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.000
When you create your SMART goal,&nbsp;
try to answer questions like:

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:59.600
• Is this achievable in the given time frame?
• What is the longest and shortest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:04.560
possible time to achieve this goal?
• What potential blockers or time-related&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.160
factors could delay progress?
• What have we accomplished&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.160 --> 00:08:12.880
in similar time frames in the past?
• When and how will we check for progress?&nbsp;

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:16.080
• What do we do if we’re&nbsp;
off-track halfway through?&nbsp;

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:20.880
• Are there any times where progress&nbsp;
might naturally slow down or speed up?

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:28.080
Example of a Time-bound SMART goal: For a simple&nbsp;
health example, consider everyone’s favorite New&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.080 --> 00:08:34.080
Year’s Resolution: “I’m going to go to the gym&nbsp;
more.” A SMART fitness goal might include defining&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:39.600
exactly how often and for how long we’ll go&nbsp;
to the gym. Instead of a very general goal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.080
you might resolve to go to the gym three days a&nbsp;
week for one hour each day, for the next month.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:49.680
Or, if you wanted to lose weight, you might&nbsp;
make a time-bound fitness goal like this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:52.240
“I’m going to lose 10 pounds&nbsp;
over the next three months.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:59.120
You might then decide on check-in milestones every&nbsp;
two weeks to stay on track. Of course, it would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:03.760
up to the rest of the SMART criteria to determine&nbsp;
other details about the how of this goal.

